//? Title: Neural Spline Flows: Positive Slopes and Analytic Inversion
//? Subtitle: Rational-quadratic bins, knot and tail fixtures, and a preserved banana-density fit
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An invertible density model needs a tractable local volume factor as well as an
inverse. A monotone rational-quadratic spline supplies both while allowing a
nonlinear change within each bin. This native C++ study first checks a two-bin
map by hand, then preserves the original four-bin fixture and four-coupling
banana experiment. Its complete program retains all 272 fixture rows, all 3,264
input/head objective partials, all 48 Adam writes, actual forward/inverse
Jacobians and all 1,024 inverse samples. The fixed-final test NLL is 2.871595860
nats per two-coordinate row, below the fitting-only full Gaussian's2.994631823
and above analytic truth's2.796480843. The source experiment is reproduced
exactly; the short two-dimensional fit does not quantify performance across
training runs or establish a full-image density benchmark.
)
\toc

> Replace an affine coordinate change with a monotone rational quotient

[Durkan, Bekasov, Murray and Papamakarios, Neural Spline Flows
(2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.04032) introduces monotone rational-quadratic
spline transformations with analytic inverses. Our migrated source uses four
small conditional spline blocks, each with four bins on[-4,4] and identity
tails. The following formulas describe the scalar map used inside each block.

For a bin from(x_k,y_k) to(x_{k+1},y_{k+1}), let w=x_{k+1}−x_k>0,
h=y_{k+1}−y_k>0, delta=h/w, theta=(x−x_k)/w, endpoint slopes d0,d1>0,
and kappa=d0+d1−2delta. Then

$$ f(x)=y_k+h\frac{\delta\theta^2+d_0\theta(1-\theta)}
{\delta+\kappa\theta(1-\theta)},\quad
f'(x)=\frac{\delta^2[d_1\theta^2+2\delta\theta(1-\theta)+d_0(1-\theta)^2]}
{[\delta+\kappa\theta(1-\theta)]^2}. $$
\alttext(The rational-quadratic value interpolates the two knot positions and positive endpoint slopes. Its derivative is a positive numerator divided by a squared positive denominator.)

For theta in[0,1], the denominator is positive: kappa>−2delta and
theta(1−theta)<=1/4 imply it exceeds delta/2. The derivative is positive,
with endpoint values d0 and d1. Adjacent bins share knot positions and slopes,
so the map and first derivative join continuously in exact arithmetic. Fixed
boundary slopes one connect to identity tails. The local log determinant is
log(f'(x)); an inverse step negates it at the recovered point.

Given y, set s=y−y_k and solve a theta²+b theta+c=0 with

$$ a=s\kappa+h(\delta-d_0),\quad b=hd_0-s\kappa,\quad
c=-\delta s,\quad
\theta=\frac{2c}{-b-\sqrt{b^2-4ac}},\quad x=x_k+w\theta. $$
\alttext(The inverse obtains its within-bin coordinate from a closed quadratic root and then recovers x from the bin's left knot and width.)

The core uses this closed root, with no numerical root search. It selects
forward bins with x edges and inverse bins with y edges. Exact interior knots
belong to the right bin; the final edge is capped at the last bin. Inclusive
endpoints use the spline, while strict outside tails return the original value
and zero logdet. The map is C1 in exact arithmetic, but log-derivative input/head
partials can have different one-sided values at knots. Structural bin choices
are stopped; derivatives follow the selected branch. The API rejects
unrepresentable bins, slopes, quadratic or derivative terms rather than hiding
an inverse fallback.

A two-coordinate coupling preserves a, uses its small conditioner to produce
spline heads, and maps only b. Its triangular Jacobian determinant is the
scalar spline derivative at b. Four alternating blocks contribute their own
per-row log determinants, followed by a learned positive diagonal scale:

$$ \log p_X(x)=-\log(2\pi)-\tfrac12\|z\|^2+
\sum_{i=0}^3\log f_i'(b_i)+\sum_{c=0}^1\mathrm{log\_scale}_c. $$
\alttext(The observation density is Gaussian latent density plus the four conditional spline log determinants and the final diagonal scaling log determinant.)

Fitting minimizes negative mean log density in nats per two-dimensional row.
Sampling supplies Gaussian z, unscales it and reverses blocks3,2,1,0, recomputing
heads on each current unchanged conditioning coordinate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spline_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=32;
 d.node("split",{-24,0},"Condition on a\n1 -> 16 tanh -> 11",s)
 .node("shift",{-11,0},"Positive bin sizes\nPositive knot slopes",s)
 .node("add",{2,0},"Choose one bin\nRational quotient",s)
 .node("stack",{15,0},"Log derivative\nClosed quadratic inverse",s)
 .node("density",{28,0},"4 coupling blocks\nGaussian density",s)
 .edge("split","shift").edge("shift","add").edge("add","stack").edge("stack","density")
 .save_svg("build/spline_method.svg");
}
