\image(figures/spline_jacobians.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Every actual forward and inverse determinant is positive. The source maximum forward logdet error is 5.06691e-7; all inverse errors also satisfy 5e-6. Pool indices have separate meanings, so this overlay does not pair corresponding points.)
\alttext(128 blue forward and 1024 orange inverse logdet residuals cluster near zero at the displayed 1e7 scale, with no omitted rows.)

> Check normalization identities and both floating roundtrips

For samples x=f⁻¹(z), log p_X(x)+log|det Df⁻¹(z)| must equal log p_Z(z).
Every one of the 1,024 generated rows is checked, independently of the density
window. This is a change-of-variables consistency check, not a finite-window
quadrature approximation. The source tolerance is 2e-5 nats; its model base
constant is rounded as described above. The following native table also retains
the maximum errors of both floating roundtrips. Inversion has an exact algebraic
form, while Float32 operations still incur rounding error.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spline_normalization.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;std::map<std::string,n::Tensor> v;
 for(auto& [k,x]:n::load_npz("build/spline_density/plot_values.npz"))v[k]=x;
 auto error=v.at("normalization_error");
 dm::graphics::RPlot::create().points(n::arange(0,1024).to_vector<double>(),(error*1e6).to_vector<double>(),"All prior rows",{37,99,235},2)
 .y_limits(0,5).x_label("Original prior row index").y_label("Absolute identity residual x 1e6, nats")
 .title("Every inverse sample satisfies change of variables")
 .save_svg("build/spline_normalization.svg");
 std::print("| Exact-form / Float32 check | Maximum absolute error |\n|---|---:|\n| inverse(forward(test)) |{:.12g}|\n| forward(inverse(prior)) |{:.12g}|\n| Change-of-variables log identity |{:.12g}|\n",n::max(v.at("forward_roundtrip_absolute_error")).item(),n::max(v.at("inverse_roundtrip_absolute_error")).item(),n::max(error).item());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Exact-form / Float32 check | Maximum absolute error |
|---|---:|
| inverse(forward(test)) |1.25169754028e-06|
| forward(inverse(prior)) |1.40070915222e-06|
| Change-of-variables log identity |1.8525259291e-06|
\image(figures/spline_normalization.svg)
// result_end

\caption(All 1,024 normalization residuals are plotted in original prior-row order, without subsampling. The checks also include bit-exact restore, repeated samples/Jacobians, single/batch parity, per-point determinant cancellation and immutable inference.)
\alttext(1024 blue absolute normalization residuals remain small at the displayed million-fold scale; no row exceeds the original 2e-5-nat tolerance.)

> What the checks establish

The complete program matches all 22 original archives(235,867 values),144 update
captures(84,336 values), batch/history TSVs, data protocol and every non-timing
metric exactly. It retains all source bins/slopes,272 fixture rows and 3,264
fixture input/head partials,48 Adam writes, actual whole-map Jacobians,1,024
inverse samples, density cells and fitting-only moments. Independent NumPy
checks159,943 forward values,43,900 parameter partials,42,144 exact updated
weights,17 model-leaf finite differences,3,192 fixture finite differences,
all raw Gaussian/banana rows and every normalization identity without calling
native model math, external autodiff or refitting a substitute.

Positive bin geometry and knot slopes establish monotonicity in exact arithmetic;
actual first derivatives, analytic log determinants and quadratic inversion
agree within the retained floating tolerances. Bin choices remain structural,
and representability checks reject arithmetic that cannot maintain these
properties. The short predefined-final fit improves on fitting-only Gaussian
baselines while remaining worse than analytic truth. That result belongs to
this 878-value, four-bin model, retained seeds and procedural pools; it is not
a tuned best model or uncertainty estimate across repeated experiments.

